<h1>The Proposal<br/>
<em>Factory</em>
</h1>
<p class="article-deck">From a good conversation to a proposal worth sending.</p>
<button class="print-link" id="print-note">Print this field note ↗</button>
</header>
<section class="section note-sheet">
<article class="reading">
<p class="eyebrow">A working instrument / Danny McGiffin</p>
<h2>Stop starting from a blank page.</h2>
<p>A proposal is a set of decisions expressed clearly. Build a small system that assembles what you already know, so you can spend your attention on the commitments you are about to make.</p>
<h2>01 / Gather the ingredients</h2>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>Conversation notes:</strong> the problem in the client’s words, why now, who is involved, and what a better result would look like.</li>
<li>
<strong>Source material:</strong> relevant examples, requirements, constraints, and existing documents you have permission to use.</li>
<li>
<strong>Your standards:</strong> the sections every proposal needs, your terms, and the decisions only you can make.</li>
</ul>
<h2>02 / Assemble the first draft</h2>
<p>Use the same structure each time. A template is enough to begin; add automation when the repeated work warrants it.</p>
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Situation.</strong> What is happening, and why does it matter?</li>
<li>
<strong>Intended result.</strong> What should be different when the work is done?</li>
<li>
<strong>Scope.</strong> What will you deliver? What is excluded?</li>
<li>
<strong>Working arrangement.</strong> Who provides what, and who decides?</li>
<li>
<strong>Timing and fee.</strong> What have you actually agreed or are proposing?</li>
<li>
<strong>Acceptance.</strong> How will both parties know the work is complete?</li>
</ol>
<h2>03 / Keep judgment human</h2>
<p>If you use AI to assemble a draft, instruct it to use only the supplied material, mark missing information as an open question, and never invent fees, dates, proof, or commitments. Use an approved tool appropriate for the information you are handling.</p>
<p>Then review every promise. Does the scope answer the problem? Are dependencies visible? Is the fee intentional? Would the client read the same meaning into each sentence?</p>
<h2>Before you send</h2>
<ul>
<li>Every claim has a source.</li>
<li>Every commitment has an owner.</li>
<li>Every assumption is visible.</li>
<li>Every open question is resolved or explicitly raised.</li>
<li>A person has reviewed and approved the whole proposal.</li>
</ul>
<h2>The machinery is working when…</h2>
<p>You spend less time assembling and more time deciding. Track time from conversation to reviewed proposal, the corrections needed, and the questions clients still ask. Improve the template at the point where the same question repeats.</p>
